## Issue-scoped coverage

- **THE-247:** The 10 selected `agent::tests` cover configured and final
  composed volume refusal before host fallback, launch effects, VPN/ensure,
  and secrets; the two selected `panes::tests` cover terminal launch and
  configured-volume refusal. The 64 core sandbox selectors include named
  volume grammar/argv and backend planning regressions. This supports the
  intended fail-closed Linux behavior. It does not claim live Docker/Podman,
  OCI, provider, or Windows runtime coverage.
- **THE-326:** All 16 selected service tests are the FRP typed-document
  family. They cover strict typed reparse/equality, invalid fields before
  secret resolution, zero ports, DNS/proxy bounds, duplicate/unknown fields,
  UTF-8 token escaping, IPv6, wire shapes, and redacted debug output. No FRP
  server/client or credentialed provider was run.
- **THE-222:** 11 `merge_remote` and 9 `platform::gate_path` selectors cover
  real local Git bundle movement, collision refusal, special-file and
  replacement preservation, write/flush/unwind cleanup, descriptor identity,
  bounded child custody, and nonrecursive cleanup. The missing hostile
  two-process race is the qualification above. The selected 20 startup
  selectors are shared existing THE-617/THE-639 coverage, not additional
  THE-222 acceptance.

## Historical failures retained

The durable package preserves the earlier strict-lint failure log and the
rejected `cargo test --keep-going --no-run` admission, rather than rewriting
them as passes. The earlier lint failure reported the unreachable return,
needless return, and collapsible-if diagnostics; the corrected final Clippy
receipt is the separate exit-0 result at the exact final source. The rejected
test-build admission is historical and is not counted among the final 145
passes; the corrected one-job build admission is the provenance for the pinned
harnesses used by the focused receipt.

## Explicit limits and separate ownership

This is Linux focused evidence. It makes no Windows runtime, live FRP/OCI,
release-performance, coverage-percentage, or full-workspace native claim.
THE-222 Windows protected owner-SID DACL, stable file identity, reparse and
sharing behavior remain source-reviewed only. Same-UID replacement between
identity observation and unlink remains an observational race; cleanup
preserves uncertain replacements rather than claiming an atomic lease.

The sibling temporary-path audit remains separate: THE-394 owns driver/fold
worktree paths, THE-224 owns sandbox secret files, THE-223 owns remote bundle
streaming/budgeting, THE-328 owns share-file materialization, and THE-325 owns
share-client credential transport. No sibling issue is silently closed by this
receipt.

No build, test, provider, native, canonical, or Linear operation was performed
by this independent review.

